! jb_af1c F214-X "MULTIPLY DOUBLE TEST", sub-block F214XB "TEST
! UNCHANGED REGS", p.143 (octal 020142.00-020165.00, the block's own
! exit "B,$+1.").  Continues fp_arith_f214xa.strap.
!
! This is D*'s answer to F214C, and it draws the line at the other end
! of the fraction.  F214C proved MULTIPLY leaves IBM 64-127 alone; p.97
! says D* writes IBM 12-107, so the open question is whether it stops
! there.  XB1 and XB2 check IBM 108-127 from both polarities:
!
!   XB1  "0 S REM 0"  -- left word FPK52, right word zeroed, sign byte
!        zeroed, then D*(U),FPK53.  R 44-63 must still be zero.
!   XB2  "1 S REM 1"  -- left word FPK52, right word all ones, sign
!        byte all ones, same D*.  R 44-63 must still be ones.
!
! "R 44-63" is the RIGHT accumulator word's bits 44-63, i.e. IBM
! 108-127 -- the twenty bits just past the product field.  The check is
! a 20-bit connective at offset 0, and offset 0 is the right word's
! right end, so it reads exactly those bits.  XB2 is the load-bearing
! one: D* genuinely writes IBM 64-107 here (FPK52 x FPK53 puts
! 539770539772 in the low half, per F214XA2), so a model that ran the
! product one word wide, or that wrote the whole right word, fails it.
!
! WHICH OPERAND EACH CONNECTIVE LOOKS AT, reading the truth table as
! the results for (memory, accumulator) = (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1):
!   CT0101 = 0,1,0,1  ->  the ACCUMULATOR bit; memory is a dummy
!   CT1010 = 1,0,1,0  ->  NOT the accumulator bit; memory is a dummy
!   CT0011 = 0,0,1,1  ->  the MEMORY bit, i.e. the sign byte at word 10
!   CT1100 = 1,1,0,0  ->  NOT the memory bit
! So the R checks read the accumulator and ignore their addressed
! constant entirely, while the SB checks read memory.
!
! THE SIGN-BYTE CHECKS SKIP BIT 4 ON PURPOSE.  They test SB 0-3 and SB
! 5-7, never bit 4, because bit 4 is the result sign and D* is entitled
! to write it.  SB 5-7 are the flag bits, so XB2 does pin something the
! arithmetic block does not: D* must carry the ACCUMULATOR's flags
! through unchanged (a model that zeroed them would leave 000 where
! XB2 demands 111).
!
! TRANSCRIPTION NOTE on XB2's first check.  The scan prints its address
! as 020157.40, which cannot be right: the D* above it is at 020156.40
! and XB1 proves D* is a half word (its own D* at 020145.00 is followed
! by a connective at 020145.40).  Taking the row as 020157.00 makes
! every one of the eleven rows below it land on its printed address,
! through B,SSW at 020166.40; taking it as .40 makes all eleven wrong.
! Transcribed as 020157.00.
!
! The listing labels this block "F214B" in its left column even though
! it sits inside F214X -- a name it already used for the MULTIPLY
! sign-modifier block.  Renamed F214XB here to keep the two apart; the
! label is only a branch target for the mode loop, which is not
! transcribed.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes: SERS (octal 1304) -> FAIL halt,
! -- SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch.
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- constants (pool p.176) --
        SLC, (8)22005.0
FPK0    DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000000000000000000000
FPK1    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777777
        SLC, (8)22071.0
FPK52   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0001024713560247135620
FPK53   DD(BU,64,8), (8)0000400000000000000040

        SLC, (8)20142.0
! F214XB1 - 0 s remain 0
F214XB  C0011(BU,64), FPK52, 64              ! left word <- FPK52
        C0000(BU,64), FPK0                   ! right word <- 0
        CM0000(BU,8), $SB
        D*(U), FPK53
        CT0101(BU,20), FPK0                  ! does R44-63 remain +0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0011(BU,4), $SB                    ! does SB0-3 remain +0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT0011(BU,3), $SB+.5                 ! does SB5-7 remain +0
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
! F214XB2 - 1 s remain 1 s
        C0011(BU,64), FPK52, 64              ! left word <- FPK52
        C1111(BU,64), FPK1                   ! right word <- all ones
        CM1111(BU,8), $SB
        D*(U), FPK53
        CT1010(BU,20), FPK1                  ! does R44-63 remain +1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1100(BU,4), $SB                    ! does SB0-3 remain +1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        CT1100(BU,3), $SB+.5                 ! does SB5-7 remain +1 s
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
PASS    BEW, $                               ! 020165.00: the block's exit "B,$+1."
        END, F214XB
